    "author_association": "MEMBER",
    "body": "### Problem\n\nPerforming an edit immediately after `{Visual}Q` performs it twice at the main cursor. This is immediately noticeable for the non-destructive edit, like \"multiply\".\n\nThe reason is most likely due to there being both extra and main cursor on the same cell, which are not yet deduplicated at the time of the edit.\n\n### Steps to reproduce\n\n1. `nvim --clean`\n2. `nnoremap gm yiwp` (\"multiply\" word at cursor)\n3. `i` - `aa` - `<CR>` - `bb` - `<Esc>` (to create text to act upon)\n4. `vip` - `Q` (to start multicursor)\n5. `gm`. This multiplies the word under main cursor twice. Moving cursor right-left (`lh`) before `gm` makes it act as expected, because extra cursor at main cursor cell is removed.\n\n### Expected behavior\n\nAn edit immediately after `{Visual}Q` is not applied twice at main cursor position. Possible approaches:\n- Do not place extra cursor at main cursor as a result of `{Visual}Q`.\n- Deduplicate cursors before performing an edit.\n\n### Nvim version (nvim -v)\n\nNVIM v0.13.0-dev-1684+g0ea627cfb7\n\n### Vim (not Nvim) behaves the same?\n\nNo\n\n### Operating system/version\n\nEndeavourOS Linux x86_64, 7.2.6-arch2-1\n\n### Terminal name/version\n\nGhostty 1.3.1-arch2\n\n### $TERM environment variable\n\nxterm-ghostty\n\n### Installation\n\nAppimage and from source",
